<article class="env-example" id="03O2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03O2">Example <span data-tag="03O2">59.15.7</span>.</a> Consider the presheaf </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \begin{matrix}  \mathcal{F} : 

&  (\mathit{Sch}/S)^{opp} 

&  \longrightarrow 

&  \textit{Ab} 

\\ &  T/S 

&  \longmapsto 

&  \Gamma (T, \Omega _{T/S}). 

\end{matrix}  \]
</div>
  <p> The compatibility of differentials with localization implies that $\mathcal{F}$ is a sheaf on the Zariski site. However, it does not satisfy the sheaf condition for the fpqc topology. Namely, consider the case $S = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{F}_ p)$ and the morphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \varphi : V = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{F}_ p[v]) \to U = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{F}_ p[u])  \]
</div>
  <p> given by mapping $u$ to $v^ p$. The family $\{ \varphi \} $ is an fpqc covering, yet the restriction mapping $\mathcal{F}(U) \to \mathcal{F}(V)$ sends the generator $\text{d}u$ to $\text{d}(v^ p) = 0$, so it is the zero map, and the diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \mathcal{F}(U) \ar[r]^{0} &  \mathcal{F}(V) \ar@<1ex>[r] \ar@<-1ex>[r] &  \mathcal{F}(V \times _ U V) }  \]
</div>
  <p> is not an equalizer. We will see later that $\mathcal{F}$ does in fact give rise to a sheaf on the étale and smooth sites. </p>
</article>